<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfred Saker</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1814">1814</a>–<a href="/years/1880">1880</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfred Saker was an English Baptist missionary who worked on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bioko">Fernando Po</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> coast from 1844. He founded the mission at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douala">Douala</a>, translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> into the Duala language and in 1858 founded the settlement of Victoria, now Limbe, after the Spanish authorities expelled Protestant missionaries from Fernando Po. He is regarded as a pioneer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in Cameroon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfred-saker.jpg" alt="Alfred Saker" width="320" height="513" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JNicolasKONDAYANSA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfred_Saker.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alfred Saker was born on 21 July 1814 at Borough Green in Wrotham, Kent, the son of a millwright and engineer. He went to the local National School and then worked in his father's workshop, building a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">steam engine</a> before he was sixteen. Baptised on 4 January 1834, he became a lay preacher in the villages around. After his father's death in 1838 he prepared drawings for the Admiralty at the Devonport dockyard, and in February 1840 he married Helen Jessup. In 1843 the Baptist Missionary Society took him on, and he sailed by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bioko">Fernando Po</a>, arriving in 1844.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Saker went out as assistant missionary and engineer. In 1845 he began work at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douala">Douala</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> coast, where he built a house with his own hands, wrote down the language of the people and printed schoolbooks on a press sent from Devonport. A church was formed there in 1849. He introduced breadfruit, mangoes and oranges and trained converts as carpenters, smiths and bricklayers. By 1851 deaths had left him the only European of the original mission, and he took the lead. He completed the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> in Duala in 1872 and printed 200 copies at his own press.</p><p>He worked closely with Jamaican missionaries, many of them formerly enslaved. Joseph Merrick had translated the Gospel of Matthew into Isubu, and Joseph Jackson Fuller wrote of tensions between the whites from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the former slaves from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>. In 1863 Saker was paid £250, Fuller £125 and the Rev. Johnson, the first person Saker baptised there, £100. Local believers and a Scottish missionary complained of his conduct; the society cleared him but said his treatment of local believers had to change, and the Scot, who held that Saker's achievements owed much to Merrick and Fuller, was dismissed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Saker left the work at the end of the 1870s and died in Peckham on 12 March 1880. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a> judged him the most important English missionary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. Saker Baptist College in Limbe, the College Alfred Saker Deïdo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douala">Douala</a> and a street there bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alfred Saker” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>British missionaries, notably <b>Alfred Saker</b> from 1845, introduced Baptist Christianity and a written Duala language.</blockquote><a href="/state/duala-kingdoms">Duala kingdoms</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Wrotham, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Peckham, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.31,0.31&amp;year=1856" aria-label="Wrotham on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4557 386 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alfred Saker"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5007 656h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5007 656h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.31,0.31&amp;year=1856">What was here in 1856? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
